{"author":{"id":"1949ffc23f11a8cb4f8c42a45c9ad241b49570df9a18687489e9ea78d87c54a3"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1778869304,"nonce":"KLWv/niqeEvpT1zB9Y6t/CjI3OQ=","message":"Components and analyses are not the only extension points. A project may need to define\nnew tasks, losses, evaluation protocols, training protocols, optimizers/schedules,\nmetrics, report transforms, artifact codecs, or data importers. Feedbax should not force\nall of these into one overloaded \"component\" registry, but they should share a common\nregistration pattern:\n\n- Stable type ID.\n- Provider/package identity and version.\n- Python import path and callable/class/builder reference.\n- Parameter schema and defaults.\n- Input/output contract.\n- Compatibility predicates.\n- Serialization and manifest representation.\n- Provenance and review metadata for project-defined or agent-created entries.\n\nThis gives agents and Mandible one conceptual model: ask Feedbax what extension types are\navailable, validate a generated spec against the relevant registry, then execute only\nthrough reviewed/importable code.\n\nLikely extension categories include:\n\n- Components: graph leaves and composite graph templates.\n- Tasks: trial generation, target/input/init semantics, and task validation.\n- Losses: loss terms, composite loss builders, regularizers, and cross-timestep losses.\n- Training protocols: named training algorithms, curricula, adversarial loops,\n  optimizers, schedules, and stopping/checkpoint policies.\n- Evaluation protocols: standardized sweeps, perturbation tests, robustness tests, and\n  matched-objective comparisons.\n- Analyses: deterministic transformations from manifests/artifacts into metrics,\n  tables, figures, or derived artifacts.\n- Metrics: reusable scalar/vector summaries that may be emitted by training, evaluation,\n  or analysis.\n- Reports and renderers: deterministic report builders, figure/table renderers, and\n  display adapters.\n- Artifact codecs/loaders: preview, validation, loading, and summarization for artifact\n  roles and media types.\n- Dataset/import adapters: project-specific importers for external data or legacy runs.\n\nControllers, plants, filters, interventions, and similar executable graph nodes should\nusually be components. Higher-level model templates should usually be composite\ncomponents or graph-spec templates. If a future extension type does not fit these\ncategories, it should still follow the same stable-ID/import-path/schema/provenance\npattern before it appears in durable manifests.\n\n\nTasks define trial generation, target/input/init semantics, and often task-specific\nvalidation or evaluation assumptions. New projects should be able to register tasks such\nas a project-specific reaching protocol, go-cue timing protocol, perturbation schedule,\nor dataset-backed task.\n\nThe current `AbstractTask` model is a strong basis for this contract: tasks generate\n`TaskTrialSpec` values with `inits`, `targets`, `inputs`, `intervene`, `timeline`, and\n`extra`, provide validation trials, own a loss function, and expose a\nTaskProtocol-compatible surface (`sample_trial`, `episode_length`, `compute_loss`).\n`TaskComponent` also adapts tasks into graph execution, currently with open-loop and\nclosed-loop modes.\n\nA task registry entry should include:\n\n- Stable task type ID, such as `feedbax.SimpleReaches` or `rlrmp.VariableLengthReach`.\n- Task class or builder import path.\n- Trial-spec schema and defaults.\n- `TaskTrialSpec` field contract: init selectors, target specs, model inputs,\n  intervention parameter specs, timeline/epoch semantics, and extra metadata.\n- Required model/plant/controller capabilities.\n- Supported modes, such as open-loop, closed-loop, batched, validation, or evaluation.\n- Compatible loss families or default loss builders, where appropriate.\n- Output/target/input/state semantics needed by analyses and reports.\n- Validation-trial behavior, seed behavior, and batch/curriculum dependencies.\n- Whether the task can be represented as a graph `TaskComponent`, and if so which mode\n  and environment callbacks are required.\n- Optional visual/workspace authoring metadata: workspace bounds, goals, targets,\n  obstacles, trajectories, event markers, editable task objects, and how those UI objects\n  lower into `TaskTrialSpec` fields.\n\nProject packages could expose tasks through an entry point:\n\n```toml\n[project.entry-points.\"feedbax.tasks\"]\nrlrmp = \"rlrmp.feedbax_tasks:register_tasks\"\n```\n\n\nLosses should also be discoverable. Project-specific loss terms are common enough that\nagents should not have to patch core Feedbax or hide them inside notebooks.\n\nThe current loss model is also a useful base: `AbstractLoss` leaves implement `term`,\n`CompositeLoss` forms weighted loss trees, and loss evaluation returns a `TermTree` of\nnamed weighted components. Studio already has a `LossTermSpec` surface with selectors,\nnorms, time aggregation, discounts, and recursive children.\n\nA loss registry entry should include:\n\n- Stable loss type ID.\n- Loss class or builder import path.\n- Parameter schema, defaults, and valid ranges.\n- Required state/history fields, target fields, model fields, and selector/probe\n  requirements.\n- Whether the loss is per-timestep, cross-timestep, terminal, streaming-compatible, or\n  requires full history.\n- Reduction behavior and metric names emitted during training.\n- `TermTree` behavior: leaf label, children/flattening behavior, weight semantics, and\n  aggregation defaults.\n- Time aggregation support: all/final/range/segment/custom, discounting, and timeline\n  dependencies.\n- Selector representation. Durable specs should not depend on anonymous Python lambdas\n  unless they are marked as unreviewed/local; they should prefer registered selectors,\n  graph probes, taps, or stable field paths.\n- Streaming-loss support, including required rolling buffer/window size for\n  cross-timestep losses.\n\nThis is especially important for cross-timestep loss families and for project-specific\nregularizers that need to be reconstructed from manifests.\n\n\nThe registry/spec design should not limit Feedbax Studio to pre-programmed task and loss\npresets. A future Studio may let users author these structures visually:\n\n- A biomechanics editor could expose a scaled workspace or plant view, such as a\n  two-link arm with editable links, muscles, insertion points, force generators, sensors,\n  or constraints.\n- A task editor could expose workspace objects such as goal points, target\n  distributions, tracking trajectories, go cues, obstacles, timing epochs, or\n  perturbation schedules.\n- A loss editor could work as an overlay on the model graph, biomechanics view, task\n  workspace, or analysis outputs. The user might select a component state, port, probe,\n  endpoint, target, or workspace object and attach a penalty such as quadratic error,\n  RMS error, effort, smoothness, jerk, epoch-masked cost, or cross-timestep regularizer.\n\nThese visual objects should lower into the same durable specs rather than becoming\nseparate hidden Studio state. A visual-authoring contract should include:\n\n- Stable IDs for selectable objects: graph nodes, ports, state fields, probes, task\n  objects, biomechanical landmarks, sensors, and targets.\n- A selector/path language that can refer to those objects without anonymous Python\n  lambdas.\n- Lowering rules from visual objects to `GraphSpec`, task specs, `TaskTrialSpec`\n  templates, loss specs, intervention specs, or structured-equation specs.\n- Validation hooks that can report unresolved selectors, incompatible shapes, invalid\n  time ranges, missing targets, or physically impossible biomechanical edits.\n- Round-trip behavior: whether a visual object can be reconstructed from the lowered\n  spec, or whether the manifest must preserve explicit UI/layout metadata.\n- Units, coordinate frames, and scaling metadata for biomechanical/workspace views.\n- Provenance for generated or agent-authored visual structures.\n\nLosses are closer to declarative equations than causal graph execution. Studio may\ntherefore represent them as structured expression trees, overlays, or equation-like\nsubgraphs rather than as ordinary causal graph nodes. That is acceptable as long as the\nloss spec still resolves to registered loss terms/builders, stable selectors, declared\ntime aggregation, and reproducible lowering into executable `AbstractLoss` objects.\n\nThis is a future-facing constraint, not an MVP requirement. The first implementation can\ncontinue to expose form-based task/loss specs, but it should not choose ID formats,\nselectors, registry schemas, or manifest layouts that would prevent richer visual\nauthoring later.\n\n\nSome extensions are not components, tasks, or losses; they are protocols for how a model\nis trained or evaluated. Examples include adversarial training loops, minimax/CVaR-style\ntraining, curriculum schedules, matched-objective evaluation protocols, or standardized\nrobustness sweeps.\n\nToday, reusable project training and analysis procedures are often exposed as modules\nresolved by `ExperimentRegistry` from a package, part, and domain (`training` or\n`analysis`). That module-key mechanism should be treated as an existing project-protocol\nregistry, but it needs more explicit schemas, declared inputs/outputs, and manifest\nemission.\n\nThese should be registered when they become reusable named procedures. A protocol entry\nshould include:\n\n- Stable protocol type ID.\n- Callable/builder import path.\n- Input spec schema.\n- Project/module key compatibility when wrapping existing `ExperimentRegistry` modules.\n- Required artifacts/manifests.\n- Emitted manifest kind and artifact roles.\n- Determinism/seed behavior.\n- Compute/review requirements, especially if it launches expensive jobs.\n- Resume/checkpoint behavior, worker/backend requirements, and status/event schema for\n  long-running jobs.\n\n\nFeedbax may also need registries for:\n\n- Report transforms: deterministic report builders over analysis/evaluation manifests.\n- Figure/table renderers: named renderers for artifacts and report products.\n- Artifact codecs/loaders: how to load, validate, summarize, or preview artifacts by\n  media type and role.\n- Dataset/import adapters: project-specific importers that turn external data into\n  Feedbax specs, artifacts, or manifests.\n\nThese can be added later, but the manifest/provider design should leave room for them.\nThe common rule is that anything referenced by a durable manifest should have a stable\ntype ID and a resolvable, reviewed implementation.\n\n\nMetrics are reusable summaries emitted during training, evaluation, or analysis. Some\nmetrics are tightly coupled to a protocol, but reusable project metrics should be\nregistered so manifests can name them consistently.\n\nA metric registry entry should include:\n\n- Stable metric type ID.\n- Callable import path or expression/builder reference.\n- Input contract: accepted manifest kinds, artifact roles, history fields, state fields,\n  target fields, or table columns.\n- Output contract: scalar/vector/table shape, dtype, units, axis labels, and display\n  name.\n- Aggregation semantics: per-trial, per-batch, per-run, per-run-set, or cross-run.\n- Directionality and interpretation, such as lower-is-better, higher-is-better, or\n  descriptive-only.\n- Missing-data behavior and validity conditions.\n- Determinism/seed behavior if the metric samples or bootstraps.\n\nMetric IDs should be used in manifests instead of ad hoc strings whenever a metric\nbecomes part of comparison, filtering, reporting, or decision-making.\n\n\nReports and renderers turn manifests and artifacts into human-facing products. They\nshould be separate from analyses when the core computation has already happened and the\nremaining work is selection, layout, rendering, or narrative assembly.\n\nA report transform entry should include:\n\n- Stable report type ID.\n- Callable import path or builder reference.\n- Input schema: accepted manifest kinds, required analysis/evaluation products, optional\n  narrative inputs, and parameter schema.\n- Output schema: emitted report manifest, artifact roles, media types, and file naming\n  conventions.\n- Determinism declaration: deterministic, deterministic-with-seed, or authored/manual.\n- Authorship/provenance rules, especially for agent-authored narrative text.\n- Compatibility predicates for project, run type, analysis family, or artifact set.\n\nA figure/table renderer entry should include:\n\n- Stable renderer type ID.\n- Accepted input artifact roles, table schemas, or metric IDs.\n- Output media types, dimensions, backend requirements, and interactive/static behavior.\n- Styling/theme parameters that affect reproducibility.\n- Whether the renderer is pure display or emits durable artifacts that should be\n  referenced by a manifest.\n\nReport manifests should distinguish deterministic report products from narrative or\nmanual products. Agent-authored narrative can be useful, but it should carry author,\nprompt/session, review, and source-manifest metadata rather than being treated as a\ndeterministic analysis result.\n\n\nArtifacts need loaders, validators, previewers, and summarizers. This should be a\nregistry because artifacts may live outside Feedbax's source tree and may be produced by\nproject-specific tools.\n\nAn artifact codec/loader entry should include:\n\n- Stable codec type ID.\n- Supported artifact roles and media types.\n- File extensions and optional magic/header checks.\n- Loader callable import path and expected return type.\n- Validator callable import path and structured error/warning format.\n- Preview/summarizer callable, if available, for Studio or Mandible graph/detail views.\n- Hashing and integrity expectations.\n- Version compatibility and migration behavior.\n- Whether loading is safe by default or requires review because it executes code or opens\n  untrusted formats.\n\nExamples include Equinox checkpoints, trajectory arrays, training histories, exported\ntables, rendered figures, and report bundles.\n\n\nDataset/import adapters turn external data, legacy runs, or project-specific layouts\ninto Feedbax specs, manifests, and artifacts. They are important for bringing older\nexperiments under the manifest-first architecture.\n\nAn importer entry should include:\n\n- Stable importer type ID.\n- Source contract: accepted paths, file patterns, database tables, remote locations, or\n  legacy project layouts.\n- Discovery behavior: whether it scans recursively, requires an explicit path, or reads\n  a manifest/index file.\n- Output contract: emitted manifest kinds, artifact roles, inferred specs, and\n  confidence/diagnostic metadata.\n- Mapping rules from legacy names/fields to Feedbax schema fields.\n- Idempotency behavior and duplicate detection.\n- Error/warning format for partially reconstructed runs.\n- Provenance fields that must be attached to imported manifests.\n- Review requirements when importer inference is lossy or ambiguous.\n\nImporters should be explicit about whether they reconstruct a faithful historical record\nor merely create a best-effort manifest around available artifacts.\n\n\n---\nCo-Authored-By: Codex (GPT-5) \u003ccodex@openai.com\u003e","files":null}]}